\image(figures/burstsort_prefix_depth.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The program explicitly logs positive node counts. The blue curve stays at log2(1)=0, while the red curve follows log2(L+1). This is a threshold effect, not evidence of a particular cache speed or an asymptotic latency measurement.)
\alttext(For eight identical strings, node count remains one at all lengths. For nine strings it grows from two nodes at length one to 513 nodes at length 512.)

The native implementation uses a flat node arena, occupied-slot records and
explicit insertion/collection stacks. Child relationships are indices, so
unwinding and destruction do not recursively follow a deep chain. Buckets
hold input ids rather than full string copies; after collection each full
string is copied once into output, followed by a final swap for the in-place
API. Tracing additionally owns the input and actual records. Parent links
avoid storing every complete node prefix; a requested prefix is materialized
in linear work in that prefix's depth.

Writing D for total input bytes, n for string count and Q for actual nodes,
routing and directory work is O(D+n+256Q), plus the cost of full-string leaf
comparisons. A leaf of k strings has O(k log(k+1)) comparison calls; the byte
cost of one call depends on those strings. Algorithm storage is
O(D+n+256Q), with O(D+n+Q) additional owned trace data. This accounting does
not claim that a root bucket or its string data necessarily fits a hardware
cache, or that the trie depth is bounded independently of input length.

> Terminals, binary bytes and failure boundaries

Ending a string is distinct from encountering byte zero. The empty string
terminates at the root; a terminates at prefix a before aa and ab are
collected. Embedded zero and high-bit bytes remain ordinary data. Sorting
is byte-lexicographic, without Unicode normalization or language collation.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::algorithms::BurstSortOptions small;small.bucket_capacity=1;
  const auto tiny=dm::algorithms::burstsort_trace({"ab","aa","a",""},small);
  if (tiny.output_input_indices!=std::vector<std::size_t>{3,2,1,0}) return 1;
  std::print("Tiny output ids: {}.\n\n| node prefix | actual terminal input ids |\n| :--- | :--- |\n",tiny.output_input_indices);
  for (std::size_t id=0;id<tiny.nodes.size();++id) {
    const auto prefix=dm::algorithms::burstsort_prefix(tiny,id);
    std::print("| {} | {} |\n",prefix.empty()?"(root)":prefix,tiny.nodes[id].terminal_input_indices);
  }
  const std::vector<std::string> binary{std::string(1,static_cast<char>(255)),"a",std::string{'\0','a'},"",std::string(1,'\0')};
  const auto bytes=dm::algorithms::burstsort_trace(binary,small);
  if (bytes.output_input_indices!=std::vector<std::size_t>{3,4,2,1,0}) return 1;
  std::print("\nBinary case sorted original ids: {}. Byte zero is not a terminator.\n",bytes.output_input_indices);
  const std::vector<std::string> deep_input(9,std::string(10000,'q'));
  const auto deep=dm::algorithms::burstsort_trace(deep_input);
  const auto prefix=dm::algorithms::burstsort_prefix(deep,deep.nodes.size()-1);
  if (deep.output!=deep_input || deep.nodes.size()!=10001 || prefix.size()!=10000) return 1;
  std::print("Deep shared prefix: {} bytes; actual nodes: {}; insertion visits: {}; directory checks: {}.\n",
      prefix.size(),deep.nodes.size(),deep.insertion_visits,deep.collection_slot_scans);
  std::vector<std::string> bounded(9,"abcd");const auto preserved=bounded;
  dm::algorithms::BurstSortOptions limit;limit.max_nodes=4;
  bool node_limit=false,zero_capacity=false,absent_node=false;
  try {dm::algorithms::burstsort_with_options(bounded,limit);}
  catch (const std::length_error&) {node_limit=true;}
  dm::algorithms::BurstSortOptions invalid;invalid.bucket_capacity=0;
  try {(void)dm::algorithms::burstsort_trace({},invalid);}
  catch (const std::invalid_argument&) {zero_capacity=true;}
  try {(void)dm::algorithms::burstsort_prefix(tiny,tiny.nodes.size());}
  catch (const std::out_of_range&) {absent_node=true;}
  if (!node_limit || !zero_capacity || !absent_node || bounded!=preserved) return 1;
  limit.max_nodes=5;dm::algorithms::burstsort_with_options(bounded,limit);
  std::print("Node cap, zero capacity and absent prefix rejected: {}, {}, {}. Failed sort preserved caller: {}.\n",
      node_limit,zero_capacity,absent_node,bounded==preserved);
  std::print("Exact five-node cap succeeds; sorted strings: {}.\n",bounded.size());
  const auto shortcut=dm::algorithms::burstsort_trace({"one"});
  std::print("Singleton shortcut: {}; actual trie nodes: {}.\n",shortcut.shortcut,shortcut.nodes.size());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Tiny output ids: [3, 2, 1, 0].

| node prefix | actual terminal input ids |
| :--- | :--- |
| (root) | [3] |
| a | [2] |

Binary case sorted original ids: [3, 4, 2, 1, 0]. Byte zero is not a terminator.
Deep shared prefix: 10000 bytes; actual nodes: 10001; insertion visits: 90009; directory checks: 2560256.
Node cap, zero capacity and absent prefix rejected: true, true, true. Failed sort preserved caller: true.
Exact five-node cap succeeds; sorted strings: 9.
Singleton shortcut: true; actual trie nodes: 0.
// result_end

A positive node cap includes the root and throws before a new child would
exceed it. No partial output is swapped into the caller's vector. Host
allocation failures likewise preserve its original values: an isolated native
test injects failures at all 103 allocation positions across default and
configured sorts, checks the unchanged input, then retries successfully.
Empty/singleton inputs preserve the original shortcut and do not fabricate
root nodes or routing work. A zero configured capacity is always rejected.

The native tests compare 19,531 exhaustive small arrays at three capacities
with stable global string order and an independent static prefix-frequency
partition. The (B+1)-th arrival sharing a prefix independently determines its
burst time, so the checker verifies actual chronological events as well as
final topology. Further tests cover 1,000 random binary/duplicate arrays,
all 256 byte slots, exact threshold and node limits, malformed prefix paths,
the 10,000-byte chain, existing distribution-sort cases and UBSan. These
explicit and generated inputs explain the mechanism without a forced dataset.

> References

- R. Sinha and J. Zobel (2004), [Cache-Conscious Sorting of Large Sets of Strings with Dynamic Tries](https://people.eng.unimelb.edu.au/jzobel/fulltext/acmjea04.pdf),
  ACM Journal of Experimental Algorithmics 9, article 1.5. The present variant
  uses index buckets, dense byte directories and full-string leaf comparisons;
  the report does not reproduce that paper's performance experiments.
- C++ working draft, [char_traits<char>](https://eel.is/c++draft/char.traits.specializations.char),
  for unsigned-byte character ordering in the standard string comparison used
  by leaf sorting.
